+# Where DOT OIG recorded administrative outcomes after an investigation
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review UTC · B129 / PD02–PD05, supporting PD01. A January 2017 DOT Inspector General letter to Congress describes a specific record trail for employee-integrity cases. It says the OIG coordinated with departmental officials about resulting administrative actions after completing and referring investigations, then recorded those actions in its **investigative database**. That is a later account of actual office practice. The corresponding outcome record for the 2006 FAA case, the governing manual and the system history remain unacquired. [DOT OIG CC2017003, physical pp. 1–2](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOT%20OIG%20response%20to%20RM%20Cummings%20et%20al%20re%20employee%20investigations%201.18.16%20FINAL.pdf#page=1)
+
+| Record | Observation and limit |
+| --- | --- |
+| **2017 congressional reply, physical p. 1 lines 37–42 and p. 2 lines 3–4** | DOT OIG describes status communication during an employee-integrity investigation. It separately describes completion, referral to the relevant DOT agency or DOJ, coordination on resulting administrative actions and entry in the investigative database. It says proactive communication may be limited when DOJ leads a potentially criminal inquiry. The letter gives no database product name, schema, case coverage or migration history. [Letter](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOT%20OIG%20response%20to%20RM%20Cummings%20et%20al%20re%20employee%20investigations%201.18.16%20FINAL.pdf#page=1) |
+| **Archived DOT 8000.1C, July 20, 1989, Chapter II** | Section 2(d) describes an investigations follow-up system. Section 3(d) describes checking management action and keeping a resolution record. Section 9(a) calls for the date, nature and support for actions taken and reasons when no action was taken. Sections 9(b) and 10 address the usual request for action notice and closing conditions. These are separate record functions. The 2017 letter supplies no cross-reference proving that its database was the same system or that the historical order governed this particular case in 2006. [Prior full-order review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/c4207143c4f0e1069991162ec8b2a398f11d5cb6/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-investigation-followup-order-2026-10-28/README.md) · [Official order, physical pp. 11–15](https://www.transportation.gov/sites/dot.gov/files/docs/dotorders/DOT8000.1C_Office%20of%20Inspector%20General%20Audit%20and%20Investigation%20Report%20Findings%2C%20Recommendations%2C%20and%20Followup%20Action_20-Jul-89.pdf#page=11) |
+| **FAA case 042R0122001 / report CC-2006-085** | The earlier TIGR case screen displays **Closed** with a start date of **12/12/2006**. The separately published report gives the correction and consideration recommendations on p. 12 and two distinct recommendations on p. 13. The closure entry supplies a date and case key. It does not supply the administrative outcome history, separate recommendation status, or delivery of a corrected answer. [Released status screen, physical p. 7](https://www.governmentattic.org/6docs/DOT-OIG-FAA9-11_2001-2006.pdf#page=7) · [Report pp. 12–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12) · [Earlier TIGR and later system comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/92199732a187c74859f43f6164912be1fc5580e6/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-tigr-case-recommendation-boundary-2026-11-05/README.md) |
+
+## Separate date and timing observations
+
+- **Letter face and library date:** both give **January 18, 2017**. **Linked PDF filename:** contains **`1.18.16 FINAL.pdf`**. These are distinct metadata observations. The earlier source file and web publication history needed to test the difference remain unavailable. [Letter face](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOT%20OIG%20response%20to%20RM%20Cummings%20et%20al%20re%20employee%20investigations%201.18.16%20FINAL.pdf#page=1) · [OIG library entry](https://www.oig.dot.gov/library-item/35451)
+- **Investigation duration versus action notice:** the letter gives **“fewer than 180 days”** for the administrative investigation goal and **“fewer than 30 days”** for the average, then uses **“within 180 days”** for the goal in a separate response. The 1989 order's §9(b) separately gives **60 days** for the usual request to report action. These values concern the stated periods and functions in their respective sources. The actual notice date, due date, governing version and scope for the FAA case are unknown. [Letter p. 2 lines 10–13 and 37–39](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOT%20OIG%20response%20to%20RM%20Cummings%20et%20al%20re%20employee%20investigations%201.18.16%20FINAL.pdf#page=2) · [Order §9, physical p. 15](https://www.transportation.gov/sites/dot.gov/files/docs/dotorders/DOT8000.1C_Office%20of%20Inspector%20General%20Audit%20and%20Investigation%20Report%20Findings%2C%20Recommendations%2C%20and%20Followup%20Action_20-Jul-89.pdf#page=15)
+- **Communication scope:** the letter reports no barriers to communication with DOT in answer 4. Answers 3(f) and 5 separately describe confidentiality and criminal-case exceptions. Preserve each answer within its stated scope. No evidence in this review links either exception to the 2006 FAA case. [Letter p. 3 lines 4–21](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/DOT%20OIG%20response%20to%20RM%20Cummings%20et%20al%20re%20employee%20investigations%201.18.16%20FINAL.pdf#page=3)
+
+## Open records
+
+- [ ] **PD02–PD03.** Locate the agency notice and supporting decision or reasons for taking no action, the investigative database outcome entries and their entry dates, and the separate recommendation responses, implementation evidence and closeout approvals for **CC-2006-085 / 042R0122001**. Compare each entry against the corresponding signed instrument. An administrative outcome might be stored separately from a referral, a closed-case flag or a recommendation response. This remains a proposed check.
+- [ ] **PD04–PD05.** Find the 2006 and 2017 investigative database names, data dictionaries, change history and case migration or archival crosswalk. Obtain the applicable manual, 2006 case classification and distribution/assignment records. They would test whether the 2017 practice identifies an actual destination for this case. Neither continued use of TIGR nor retroactive entry has been verified.
+- [ ] **PD01.** Obtain the corrected May 2003 answer, attachments, transmittal and recipient acknowledgment. Compare the version and date to any completed-action notice. Keep recommendations **1–2** separate from **3–4**. [Existing acquisition queue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/OPEN-RECORDS.md)
+
+The public OIG letter was retrieved as a **62,798-byte** four-page PDF with SHA-256 **35b98aeef08214197cca99f8bfa18b0b5baf9a86c73ecc37671b28a8f3f96ac8**. The review checked 11 text anchors and visually read relevant regions of pp. 1–3. It rehashed the previously inspected 15-page order from its preserved archived copy and reused the cited case-status observations. The PDF face and URL filename were observed separately. The review supplies a database and custody lead. No case-specific notice, decision, corrected answer or delivery evidence was found in the sources examined. [Exact source pins and reading scope](SOURCE-PINS.json)
